<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
        <artifactId>rains-flexible-modules</artifactId>
        <version>1.0.0</version>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>yeuksmp</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>YeukSMP</name>
    <description>
        One plugin carrying eight modules — claims, teleport requests, warps, moderation, the server
        pack, coloured names, homes and random teleport — for a server that wants all of them and
        would otherwise install eight jars that each have to be updated separately.

        Nothing here but a build file and a descriptor. The wrapper already knows what to do with a
        jar holding more than one module: ModuleHosts.standalone reads the module count and gives
        each one a subfolder rather than letting eight fight over one config.yml.
    </description>

    <properties>
        <!-- The name the server sees, and the name of its data folder. -->
        <plugin.name>YeukSMP</plugin.name>

        <!--
          1.0.0: the first release of the bundle. It is deliberately its own version line rather than
          the highest of the six — a bundle that claimed 2.9.0 because moderation is 2.9.0 would say
          nothing about the other five, and a bundle whose number moved every time any module moved
          would be a number nobody could read. What each module is running is in the boot log, one
          line per module, straight out of its own ModuleInfo.

          1.0.1: tpa fix — asking somebody for the first time was reported back as "already asked".
          requests.put() called right after requests.displacedBy() found the request displacedBy()
          had just stored and read it as asking the same person twice; the two are one operation now
          (TpaRequests.ask()).

          1.0.2: claims — added /claimadmin alignvisitors, which makes every claim treat visitors
          exactly as it treats trusted players for every audience-aware flag. Existing claims could
          carry a visitor override stricter than their trusted one (set on purpose, or inherited from
          data older than the two tiers being kept separate), which is what made "visitors may not
          pick items up" a server-wide surprise rather than one claim's own choice.

          1.0.3: claims — being an operator, or holding any staff rank, no longer waives the claim
          limit, the creation/fence costs or a no-claim zone by itself. Those were their own permission
          nodes defaulting to OP, and the Admin staff rank granted them outright — so every operator and
          every promoted Admin got them for free forever, with nothing in a log to show for it. All
          three now read /claimadmin bypass's own explicit, revocable toggle instead, which rec.admin
          already reaches through rec.bypass. rec.maxclaims.unlimited is untouched: a genuine claim-count
          perk, not a protection bypass.

          1.0.4: claims — the claim list's "mark out a new claim" button is now in the toolbar, dead
          centre and directly above the manual (which stays in the danger slot, unchanged), so it is
          reachable whether this is somebody's first claim or their sixth, not only while the list was
          empty. The empty state is a cobweb saying so now, rather than a second copy of the same button.

          1.0.5: tpa — being asked now comes with clickable [Accept] [Deny] buttons under the message,
          not only the commands. Two things had to happen for that to work rather than merely compile:
          TpaRequestService had to build the buttons through Core's ChatButtons, and — the part that
          would have made every button on the server silently dead — ModuleBootstrap now registers the
          rcclick command those buttons run, and ModulePlugin tells ChatButtons that command exists.
          Exactly the failure ModuleBootstrap's own class comment already warned about.

          1.0.6: claims — being trusted, and having a permission granted or taken back through the
          member screen, now tells the affected player directly (if they are online), rather than only
          the person doing the trusting or granting. Previously the only way to find out was walking
          into the claim and noticing what did or did not refuse you.

          1.0.7: claims — a trial-chamber spawner's mobs now read SPAWNER_SPAWNING like an ordinary
          spawner does, rather than being governed by nothing at all (its own SpawnReason fell through
          every check). Fixed alongside a bulk audit against the old standalone plugin: /claim's and
          /claimadmin's declared permissions matched the wrong node names (rainsclaims.* instead of
          rec.*, which only affected the /commands directory, not real access), /claim claim was
          dropped as an alias for making a new claim, and tab completion for both commands was missing
          several real subcommands and never filtered by what was already typed. Added /claimadmin here,
          which reports everything about the claim underfoot — shape, Y-range, members, bans, entry fee,
          bank, and every enforced flag's policy/override/verdict per tier — for tracking down exactly
          this kind of "the flag is off but it's still happening" report. Also: shift-clicking food (or
          potions, or equipment) from your own inventory straight into a claim's store now works, instead
          of only the one dedicated "deposit what's in your hand" button.

          1.0.8: claims — the real cause behind two separate "the flag is off but it still happens"
          reports turned out to be the same thing both times: the protection bypass toggle
          (/claimadmin bypass) silently staying on long after whoever flipped it had forgotten about
          it, which for a player-targeted flag like monster targeting overrides their own claim's
          setting entirely. The bypass itself is correct and stays — plenty of admin work genuinely
          takes a while — but it is never silent again: after ten minutes it asks in chat, with a
          button to keep going another ten minutes and a button to stop being asked for that session.
          Separately: onSpawn's own flagDenied() no longer suspends MONSTER_SPAWNING just because a
          bypassing admin happens to be standing nearby watching mobs spawn — spawning is the world
          acting on its own, not the admin doing something that would otherwise be refused, so their
          presence must not turn monster control off for everyone else near them.

          1.0.9: moderation — a report's staff-chat notification went to whoever held the reports
          permission, which does not stop being true of the person the report is about. A trial mod
          reporting a mod, or any staff reporting staff, would see themselves named in the very line
          meant to tell everyone else. Both notification points (a report filed, a report claimed) now
          exclude the subject unconditionally.

          1.0.10: claims — added /claimadmin transfer <owner/claim> <new owner>, which hands a claim to
          somebody else entirely: for an abandoned claim, or one made in the wrong person's name. Not
          the same door as /claim owner add — that deliberately cannot touch the primary owner, so a
          co-owner can never push the original owner out from under them. Transfer replaces the whole
          ownership list outright, and takes the new owner off the member list and any ban on them with
          it, so the claim they now own does not still list them as merely trusted or bar them from it.

          1.0.11: core — the whole "Looking Inside" (invsee) settings page did nothing. All four
          settings (whether it works at all, whether editing is allowed, whether armour/off-hand may
          be touched, whether the ender chest is offered) were declared and shown in the config but
          Inventories never once read any of them — switching "Looking inside inventories" off changed
          nothing a player could see. All four are now wired: the master switch actually refuses
          /invsee, the other three cap what a window offers rather than only what a permission does.
          Note left for later: EDIT_EVERYTHING (armour/off-hand editing) has no permission node that
          ever requests it, so the "allow editing worn armour" setting has nothing to turn off yet on
          its own — that is a second, separate piece of work in the moderation module.

          1.0.12: claims — the member screen had a button to add a co-owner and nothing to trust
          somebody without going that far, so the only way to do the equivalent of /claim trust was
          the command itself. Added "Trust somebody" next to it: pick a player, they are added exactly
          as /claim trust adds them, and they are told about it if online, same as the command already
          does.

          1.0.13: claims — handing a claim over entirely used to be an admin-only door
          (/claimadmin transfer), so an owner giving up their own claim had no way to do it themselves —
          only a co-owner (via /claim owner add), which deliberately cannot touch the primary owner at
          all. Added /claim transfer <player>, open to any owner or co-owner of the claim underfoot, same
          as delete already is: it replaces the whole ownership list with the one player named, exactly
          like the admin version, and tells them if they are online.

          1.0.14: claims — /claim transfer's click equivalent. The member screen's danger slot now offers
          "Hand this claim over": pick who, confirm (this one asks first, unlike the command), and it goes
          through exactly as /claim transfer does, telling the new owner if they are online.

          1.0.15: claims — three ways a ban stopped meaning what it says. Riding a boat, horse or Happy
          Ghast across a border never ran the ban/entry gate at all, because PlayerMoveEvent does not fire
          for a vehicle's passenger — only VehicleMoveEvent does, and nothing listened for it; a banned
          rider could sit inside a claim they were refused on foot. A refused crossing while gliding on an
          elytra cancelled the move but never resynced the client's predicted trajectory, leaving the
          player hanging in the air at the line, unable to glide on, fall or do anything else. And logging
          back in while banned, standing in or right on the border of the claim, was never checked at
          all — fixed by evicting on join the same way /claim ban already evicts someone caught inside,
          deliberately not by refusing the login itself, which would have turned a claim ban into one
          nobody could ever lift.

          1.1.0: added homes and random teleport, and moderation gained two automated watchers.
          Homes was ported from the third-party SetHome plugin (see homes-module's own history) and
          is no longer deliberately excluded — a server asking for this bundle now gets it, sethome
          and delhome included, importing an existing SetHome installation's homes on a first run the
          same way the standalone plugin does. Random teleport is new: /rtp (wild, randomtp), a ring
          around spawn or the player with a configurable minimum and maximum, and a landing whose
          safety is the owner's policy — available to the player, forced either way, or off
          entirely, in which case a checked-off /rtp is exactly a random point with no net under it,
          void included. Moderation's own two: typing a watched command (seedcracker and its typos,
          by default) and a mining pattern that looks like x-ray both now file an automatic report
          into the same queue /report already uses, the x-ray threshold able to learn what this
          server's own players actually mine rather than one fixed number for every server. And
          fixed here, in the wrapper every module in this jar shares: /settings itself was never
          registered by anything — every module could be reached through its own /<name> config
          subcommand, but the command a player would actually type for "everything on this server"
          simply did not exist on any module-standalone plugin or bundle in this repository.

          1.2.0: /rtp with a checked landing could take the better part of a minute to actually go —
          RainsCore's search for solid ground was walking down one block at a time from high above
          the target, and every one of those steps itself scanned up to ninety-six blocks looking for
          a landing while there was still nothing solid below: quadratic in the distance fallen, over
          open air the search always had to fall through first. Fixed in RainsCore (1.8.0, see its own
          history): the search now seeds itself from the world's own heightmap — one lookup instead of
          a walk — and a neighbour's height is checked in a narrow window instead of a second full
          walk of its own. Measured on the same terrain that used to take the better part of a minute:
          862 block reads where it used to take 190,285. A hard two-second ceiling backs that up
          regardless of how uncooperative the terrain turns out to be, and /rtp now shows "Looking for
          somewhere safe to land…" on the action bar for as long as the search actually takes, so the
          wait — a couple of seconds at the very most now — no longer reads as a frozen command.

          1.3.0: a scattered arrival could land at the top of a tree, on a roof, or on any other
          solid thing merely because it was the first thing the search found — fixed in RainsCore
          (1.9.0, see its own history) with a natural-ground-only check /rtp now asks for, so a
          checked landing only ever counts stone, dirt, sand and the rest of the terrain itself as
          somewhere to actually put a player. Two further RainsCore fixes ride along: the search
          could touch Bukkit's block data off the thread that owns it whenever the chunks it needed
          were still loading, which Paper and Folia both call undefined behaviour rather than merely
          slow — it now always hops back first. And a heightmap that missed near the sky (an
          overhang, a floating structure) fell back to the exact quadratic walk the heightmap seeding
          exists to avoid, starting from wherever the search began rather than from the heightmap's
          own — if wrong — answer; it now starts there instead.

          1.4.0: /rtp can now keep a pool of already-checked landings ready, so most trips skip the
          search entirely instead of paying for it live. A daily job tops every world up to a
          configurable minimum (40 by default), an owner can ask for more by hand with
          /rtp prepare, and the whole thing has a ceiling (3000 by default) and an off switch, all in
          /settings. Nobody gets sent to the same prepared spot twice, though a spot stays in the pool
          for everybody else once used. And because the ground under a prepared spot can change
          between being found and being handed out — somebody builds on it, a tree grows, a chunk
          regenerates — every one is checked again, on the spot, the instant it is actually given to a
          player, before the search that filled the pool is ever trusted.

          1.5.0: kelp and seagrass — and any waterlogged block — were being counted as ordinary
          passable air rather than water, because Bukkit's own isPassable() answers "does this stop
          you walking" and not "is this dry"; a checked landing could put a player standing on the
          sea floor believing it had found dry ground. Fixed in RainsCore (1.10.0, see its own
          history), where the same fix closes a real, if narrow, race on every plugin disable: a read
          from Core's own databases genuinely in flight at the exact moment the server stopped could
          leave a write-ahead log unfolded, logged as a warning on the next start for no reason
          anyone watching the console could see. Two smaller things ride along: /rtp's own settings
          no longer share four key names with /rtp:warps' (rtp-warmup-seconds and the like), and a
          checked trip that rolls a point with nothing safe nearby quietly tries a few more times
          before actually refusing, since that is a fact about the one point and not about the world.
          /rtp <distance> lets a player ask for at least that many blocks from the middle for one
          trip, without an owner having to widen the ring for everybody to get it.

          1.6.0: homes.bypass.warmup and homes.bypass.cooldown defaulted to operators-only, so every
          operator skipped both waits no matter what homes' own "operators skip the waits" setting
          said — which defaults to off, on purpose, so an admin testing the feature is not the one
          person who cannot. Fixed in homes-module: both nodes now default to nobody, the same as
          the identical setting in tpa-module already had it, which is what makes the setting the
          only thing deciding for an operator with nothing explicitly granted.

          1.7.0: /rtp — a checked landing's fallback search, the one that runs when the world's
          heightmap has no standing room within a few blocks of what it reported, used to walk the
          entire column with nothing stopping it: a thick canopy or a cave mouth is exactly when that
          happens, and the walk would tunnel straight through solid rock into whatever cave it found
          first, however deep. Fixed in RainsCore (1.12.0, see its own history), bounded now to a sane
          distance from the heightmap's own answer. Landings also prefer a grass block over sand,
          gravel or bare stone, and a spot with clear sky above over one under a tree's canopy, when a
          checked search has a choice between otherwise equally good spots — never a reason to widen
          the search or refuse a ring, only a tie-break. And the location pool tops itself up by one
          more after every trip that uses it, not only once a day, so a busy server stays ahead of it
          rather than falling back to a live search until the next day's job runs.

          Also: an admin taking one permission away from a staff member by hand, through the rank
          screen's per-person toggle, used to come straight back the next time that person logged in —
          the join top-up that hands out a node a preset has gained since somebody's promotion was
          fully re-applying the whole preset, wiping every individual toggle rather than only adding
          what was actually new. It now remembers which nodes were explicitly refused and leaves them
          refused; only "put the preset back" on the rank screen, the deliberate full reset, still
          undoes those on purpose.

          1.7.1: /vanish — a vanished player's own tablist stopped receiving updates at all for as
          long as they stayed hidden, because the header/footer's recipient list and its
          vanish-filtered count came from the same list — the one person excluded from it (the
          vanished one) never got another packet. Their own tablist froze on whatever was true the
          moment they vanished, and coming back simply resumed updates from there, which read as
          nothing having changed. Fixed in RainsCore (1.13.0, see its own history): everybody online
          is sent a fresh header and footer every refresh now, hidden or not — only the count and the
          per-world tallies inside it stay filtered, for every screen including the vanished player's
          own. Alongside it: revealing somebody who is currently in Creative or Spectator mode could
          strip their flight — Spectator's client always flies regardless of the ability flag, so
          taking it away there only desynced server and client and dropped a revealed moderator
          through the world; Creative owns its own flight as a fact about the gamemode, which a
          reveal driven by vanish's own bookkeeping had no business overruling. Neither gamemode is
          touched by this any more.

          1.7.2: /vanish now also silences the player, not only hides them — footsteps, splashing,
          drinking and taking damage used to play exactly as loud whether or not anybody nearby
          could see who they belonged to, which is not actually hidden, only unseen. Fixed in
          RainsCore (1.14.0, see its own history) with vanilla's own Silent flag. A block a vanished
          player mines is untouched by this and still sounds as loud as ever — that sound belongs to
          the block, not the player.

          1.7.3: /mod <player> shows playtime, first-joined, last-seen and a handful of combat
          statistics now, read straight from Minecraft's own per-player records. A persisted x-ray
          probability rides alongside it — a long-running average of both the ore ratio and the
          mining-directness signal, surviving a restart, shown on the player's own page and ranked
          across everybody who has ever mined anything on a new /mod xray leaderboard. Clicking
          anybody on it opens the same block-by-block review a report's own investigation would, so
          the number is never the last thing anybody sees before deciding what to do with it.

          1.7.4: two things fixed in RainsCore (1.16.0, see its own history). Vanish silences a
          player's own advancement broadcast now — "so-and-so has reached the goal" used to go out
          to the whole server the moment a vanished player earned one, which announced them exactly
          as loudly as being seen would have; they keep the advancement, the server just never finds
          out. Claims also gained a new flag, equipment durability: on lets an arena hand out a
          reusable kit — armour, elytra, swords, tools, all of it — that never wears out for anybody,
          or an owner spare only their own gear while a visitor's still wears down as normal.

          1.7.5: /mod xray's review screen used to lose every found ore on a restart even though the
          probability survived one — a moderator could see "72%" and open the review to find nothing
          behind it. The actual findings are persisted now too, capped per player. The leaderboard
          also only lists players who have actually been found mining a watched ore, rather than
          anybody who has ever broken a single block of anything. And "which blocks count as
          valuable" has a proper picker now — /mod xray → Watched ores — a grid of nineteen common
          ores to click through instead of typing material names into chat.

          1.7.6: ore already exposed on two or more faces before anybody touched it — a lush cave, a
          ravine wall, an ordinary cavern — no longer counts as an x-ray find at all: both the ratio
          and the mining-directness signal assume ore reached by digging through rock that hid it, and
          ore somebody simply walked up to was never hidden. A player who stumbled onto an exposed vein
          exploring a cave no longer gets reported for it. Alongside it, a new "VeinMiner mode" setting
          (/mod xray, or xray.veinminer-mode in config) treats every same-material ore block broken
          within a quarter second of the last one credited to the same player as the tail of one
          vein-mining click rather than a separate find, so a server running a VeinMiner-style plugin
          no longer turns one deposit into a wall of separate suspicious events.
        -->
        <plugin.version>1.7.6</plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
          The eight the server asked for, and deliberately not farm worlds: a bundle is a decision
          about what this server runs, not a shortcut for "everything in the repository". Adding one
          later is adding a dependency here — ServiceLoader finds it, the shade merges the service
          files, and no list anywhere else has to be edited.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>claims-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>tpa-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>warp-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>moderation-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>pack-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>names-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>homes-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>rtp-module</artifactId>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>de.raindancer.modules</groupId>
            <artifactId>modules-wrapper</artifactId>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <finalName>${plugin.name}-${plugin.version}</finalName>
        <resources>
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
                <!-- The descriptor names the version, and a version written twice is one that drifts. -->
                <filtering>true</filtering>
            </resource>
        </resources>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
            </plugin>
            <!--
              BundleJarTest reads the jar this build produces, so it has to run after it exists.
              Surefire bound to a later phase rather than failsafe, which is one more plugin to have
              cached — this build has to work offline.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>default-test</id>
                        <phase>none</phase>
                    </execution>
                    <execution>
                        <id>after-the-jar-exists</id>
                        <phase>integration-test</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>test</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <skipTests>false</skipTests>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
